  "content": {
    "id": "p36-cc-1-11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "1",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p36-cc-instruction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "True or False?",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p36-cc-1-11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "The maximum value of $a\\cos x+b\\sin x$ is $R=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. The auxiliary angle form has amplitude $R=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$, so its maximum value is $R$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p36-cc-1-11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "In $R\\cos(x-\\alpha)$, the maximum occurs at $x=\\alpha$ and equals $R$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. At $x=\\alpha$, $\\cos(x-\\alpha)=\\cos 0=1$, so the value is $R$, its maximum.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p36-cc-1-11-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Given $a\\cos x+b\\sin x=R\\cos(x-\\alpha)$, where $a\\ne0$ and $b\\ne0$, then $\\tan\\alpha=\\dfrac{b}{a}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Equating coefficients gives $R\\cos\\alpha=a$ and $R\\sin\\alpha=b$. Therefore\n$$\\begin{align*}\\tan\\alpha&=\\frac{R\\sin\\alpha}{R\\cos\\alpha}\\\\\\tan\\alpha&=\\frac{b}{a}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p36-cc-1-11-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Given $a\\cos x+b\\sin x=R\\cos(x-\\alpha)$, where $a\\ne0$ and $b\\ne0$, then $\\alpha=\\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\dfrac{b}{a}\\right)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. You must check the correct quadrant.",
          "worked": "False. Equating coefficients gives $\\cos\\alpha=\\frac{a}{R}$ and $\\sin\\alpha=\\frac{b}{R}$. Their signs determine the quadrant of $\\alpha$; $\\tan^{-1}(\\frac{b}{a})$ alone may give an angle in the wrong quadrant.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p37-cc-1-11-part-5",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "If $a\\cos x+b\\sin x$ can be written in the form $R\\cos(x-\\alpha)$, then it cannot be written in the form $R\\sin(x-\\beta)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. The same expression can have both cosine and sine auxiliary-angle forms, since\n$$R\\cos(x-\\alpha)=R\\sin\\left(x-\\alpha+\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$$\nTake $\\beta=\\alpha-\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p37-cc-1-11-part-6",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "If $a=0$ and $b>0$, then $a\\cos x+b\\sin x=b\\cos\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. With $a=0$, the left-hand side is $b\\sin x$. Expanding the right-hand side gives\n$$\\begin{align*}b\\cos\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)&=b\\left(\\cos x\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{2}+\\sin x\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\\\b\\cos\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)&=b\\sin x.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p37-cc-1-11-part-7",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "The auxiliary angle transformation can absorb a $\\cos 2x$ term into $R\\cos(x-\\alpha)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. Expanding $R\\cos(x-\\alpha)$ gives $R\\cos\\alpha\\cos x+R\\sin\\alpha\\sin x$. These terms have argument $x$, so a nonzero $\\cos 2x$ term cannot be absorbed into this form.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p37-cc-1-11-part-8",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "If $a\\cos x+b\\sin x=R\\cos(x-\\alpha)$ where $a,b>0$ and $R>0$, then $\\alpha$ is in the first quadrant.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Expanding the cosine difference and equating coefficients gives $R\\cos\\alpha=a$ and $R\\sin\\alpha=b$. Since $a,b,R>0$, both $\\cos\\alpha$ and $\\sin\\alpha$ are positive, so $\\alpha$ is in the first quadrant.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p37-cc-1-11-part-9",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "i",
        "prompt": "If $a\\cos x+b\\sin x=R\\cos(x+\\alpha)$ where $a,b>0$ and $R>0$, then $\\alpha$ is in the first quadrant.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. Expanding the cosine sum and equating coefficients gives $R\\cos\\alpha=a$ and $-R\\sin\\alpha=b$. Thus $\\cos\\alpha>0$ and $\\sin\\alpha<0$, so $\\alpha$ is in the fourth quadrant.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p37-cc-1-11-part-10",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "j",
        "prompt": "If $a\\cos x+b\\sin x=R\\sin(x+\\alpha)$ where $a,b>0$ and $R>0$, then $\\alpha$ is in the first quadrant.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Expanding the sine sum and equating coefficients gives $R\\sin\\alpha=a$ and $R\\cos\\alpha=b$. Both $\\sin\\alpha$ and $\\cos\\alpha$ are positive, so $\\alpha$ is in the first quadrant.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p37-cc-1-11-part-11",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "k",
        "prompt": "If $a\\cos x+b\\sin x=R\\sin(x-\\alpha)$ where $a,b>0$ and $R>0$, then $\\alpha$ is in the first quadrant.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. Expanding the sine difference and equating coefficients gives $-R\\sin\\alpha=a$ and $R\\cos\\alpha=b$. Thus $\\sin\\alpha<0$ and $\\cos\\alpha>0$, so $\\alpha$ is in the fourth quadrant.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
